1
00:00:01,202 --> 00:00:03,971
* In the not-too-distant 
 future *

2
00:00:03,971 --> 00:00:06,840
* Way down in Deep 13 *

3
00:00:06,840 --> 00:00:09,977
* Dr. Forrester 
 and TV's Frank *

4
00:00:09,977 --> 00:00:13,181
* Were hatching 
 an evil scheme *

5
00:00:13,181 --> 00:00:16,117
* They hired a temp 
 by the name of Mike *

6
00:00:16,117 --> 00:00:19,420
* Just a regular Joe 
 they didn't like *

7
00:00:19,420 --> 00:00:22,256
* Their experiment 
 needed a good test case *

8
00:00:22,256 --> 00:00:24,458
* So they conked him 
 on the noggin *

9
00:00:24,458 --> 00:00:25,926
* And they shot him 
 into space *

10
00:00:25,926 --> 00:00:28,662
* Get me down! *

11
00:00:28,662 --> 00:00:31,899
* We'll send him
cheesy movies *

12
00:00:31,899 --> 00:00:34,968
- * The worst we can find *
- * La la la *

13
00:00:34,968 --> 00:00:38,439
* He'll have to sit 
 and watch them all *

14
00:00:38,439 --> 00:00:40,241
* And we'll monitor 
 his mind *

15
00:00:40,241 --> 00:00:41,675
* La la la *

16
00:00:41,675 --> 00:00:44,478
* Now keep in mind, 
 Mike can't control *

17
00:00:44,478 --> 00:00:46,147
* Where the movies 
 begin or end *

18
00:00:46,147 --> 00:00:47,481
* La la la *

19
00:00:47,481 --> 00:00:50,584
* He'll try to keep 
 his sanity *

20
00:00:50,584 --> 00:00:53,921
* With the help 
 of his robot friends *

21
00:00:55,656 --> 00:00:57,057
* Robot Roll Call *

22
00:00:57,057 --> 00:00:58,592
- * Cambot * 
- Show yourself.

23
00:00:58,592 --> 00:01:00,027
- * Gypsy * 
- I'm not ready.

24
00:01:00,027 --> 00:01:01,762
- * Tom Servo! * 
- Hello there!

25
00:01:01,762 --> 00:01:03,464
- * Croooow! * 
- That's one "o".

26
00:01:03,464 --> 00:01:06,100
* If you're wondering 
 how he eats and breathes *

27
00:01:06,100 --> 00:01:09,370
- * And other science facts * 
- * La la la *

28
00:01:09,370 --> 00:01:12,406
* Just repeat to yourself, 
 "It's just a show, *

29
00:01:12,406 --> 00:01:15,343
* I should really 
 just relax *

30
00:01:15,343 --> 00:01:20,748
* For 'Mystery Science 
 Theater 3000!'" *

31
00:01:33,627 --> 00:01:36,797
Yes, yes, yes,
yes, I see, yes.

32
00:01:36,797 --> 00:01:39,667
Describe your childhood
to me, Mr. T-Robot.

33
00:01:39,667 --> 00:01:41,335
I didn't have a childhood.

34
00:01:41,335 --> 00:01:42,703
Hmm, I see.

35
00:01:42,703 --> 00:01:44,638
How would you describe
your relationship
with your mother?

36
00:01:44,638 --> 00:01:46,073
I didn't have a mother.

37
00:01:46,073 --> 00:01:48,876
Ahh, interesting
Have you seen "Cliffhanger?"

38
00:01:48,876 --> 00:01:51,044
- No.
- Hmm, tell me more about that.

39
00:01:51,044 --> 00:01:54,081
- I didn't see "Cliffhanger."
- This seems painful to you.

40
00:01:54,081 --> 00:01:58,352
- No.
- Now tell me, Crow T,
what kind of fishing lure

41
00:01:58,352 --> 00:02:00,554
do you think is appropriate
for early spring?

42
00:02:00,554 --> 00:02:01,722
Definitely
a Rebel Deep--

43
00:02:01,722 --> 00:02:05,559
nothing leaves
this office, right?

44
00:02:05,559 --> 00:02:10,097
Now, Mr. Robot,
everything said here today
is strictly confidential.

45
00:02:10,097 --> 00:02:13,334
Definitely a Rebel
Deep Diving Lure
for early spring.

46
00:02:13,334 --> 00:02:15,869
Yes, yes.

47
00:02:15,869 --> 00:02:19,307
Ah, well, I see our
time is up, Crow.
We'll be right back.

48
00:02:19,307 --> 00:02:22,176
- What?
- I'm prescribing a series
of commercials.

49
00:02:22,176 --> 00:02:23,311
( groans )

50
00:02:23,311 --> 00:02:26,180
**

51
00:02:29,250 --> 00:02:30,618
Gee, I feel
kind of embarrassed

52
00:02:30,618 --> 00:02:33,086
about the things
I told you in our session.

53
00:02:33,086 --> 00:02:34,588
Don't worry, Crow,
as I said before,

54
00:02:34,588 --> 00:02:36,590
whatever you tell me
in the privacy of my office

55
00:02:36,590 --> 00:02:38,492
is in the strictest
of confidence.

56
00:02:38,492 --> 00:02:40,093
Hi, guys.

57
00:02:40,093 --> 00:02:41,262
Say, Mike,
you won't believe

58
00:02:41,262 --> 00:02:42,663
some of the weird stuff
this nutcup is into here.

59
00:02:42,663 --> 00:02:44,164
- Hey!
- Did you know

60
00:02:44,164 --> 00:02:46,467
that he uses
a Rebel Deep Diving
Lure in early spring?

61
00:02:46,467 --> 00:02:50,771
- I lied about that.
Shh, shh! Hey, hey!
- ( laughing )

62
00:02:57,177 --> 00:02:59,513
You'll have to excuse me.

63
00:02:59,513 --> 00:03:01,782
Things have been a little
hectic down here this week.

64
00:03:01,782 --> 00:03:04,352
You see, my mother's
coming to visit,

65
00:03:04,352 --> 00:03:07,755
and, well, we've
redecorated in her honor.

66
00:03:07,755 --> 00:03:10,057
She is the one person
in my life

67
00:03:10,057 --> 00:03:13,060
who's responsible
for my deep psychological scar

68
00:03:13,060 --> 00:03:16,530
and naturally
I wanted the place
to look nice for her.

69
00:03:16,530 --> 00:03:19,400
I'm going to send you along
some material

70
00:03:19,400 --> 00:03:23,637
that I want you to say
to her when she gets here.

71
00:03:23,637 --> 00:03:26,807
You do a good job
and I'll show you
my appreciation

72
00:03:26,807 --> 00:03:28,476
by not killing you.

73
00:03:28,476 --> 00:03:32,012
- Frank.
- Relax, Clay,
everything's under control.

74
00:03:32,012 --> 00:03:34,448
Look, I made your mother
this nice fruit bowl.

75
00:03:34,448 --> 00:03:37,050
No oranges, though.
You mother can't
have oranges.

76
00:03:37,050 --> 00:03:39,186
The citric acid
gives her gas.

77
00:03:39,186 --> 00:03:41,422
That's a very good point,
Frank, beca--

78
00:03:41,422 --> 00:03:43,657
How do you know
so much about my mother?

79
00:03:43,657 --> 00:03:47,295
I've been corresponding
with her regularly for
quite some time now.

80
00:03:47,295 --> 00:03:48,762
I thought you knew that.

81
00:03:48,762 --> 00:03:49,863
- No.
- ( doorbell rings )

82
00:03:49,863 --> 00:03:52,566
That's her.
Come in.

83
00:03:56,504 --> 00:03:59,440
- Mother.
- It is so wonderful
to see you.

84
00:04:01,509 --> 00:04:04,545
( smooching )

85
00:04:04,545 --> 00:04:07,581
You look fabulous.

86
00:04:07,581 --> 00:04:09,983
Thank you
for the lovely card.

87
00:04:09,983 --> 00:04:12,219
That was so
thoughtful of you.

88
00:04:12,219 --> 00:04:14,187
We have so much
to talk about.

89
00:04:14,187 --> 00:04:15,789
Before we talk about
anything else,

90
00:04:15,789 --> 00:04:17,291
there is one thing
I have got to tell you about.

91
00:04:17,291 --> 00:04:20,594
Wait, let me guess.

92
00:04:20,594 --> 00:04:22,830
Meg Anderson's party.

93
00:04:22,830 --> 00:04:25,599
( laughing )

94
00:04:25,599 --> 00:04:27,901
Well, you're not
gonna believe it,

95
00:04:27,901 --> 00:04:30,137
- but I wore the blue dress.
- You didn't?!

96
00:04:30,137 --> 00:04:32,005
I most certainly did.
And you should have
seen when I--

97
00:04:32,005 --> 00:04:34,207
- Mother!
- What?

98
00:04:34,207 --> 00:04:38,712
Some friends of mine
have put together a little
presentation in your honor.

99
00:04:38,712 --> 00:04:39,913
I told them not to,

100
00:04:39,913 --> 00:04:43,016
but they're a bunch
of knuckleheads.

101
00:04:43,016 --> 00:04:45,218
Well, you know,
let's watch, huh?

102
00:04:45,218 --> 00:04:47,421
Hmm? ( groans )

103
00:04:47,421 --> 00:04:49,557
"Hi, Clayton.

104
00:04:49,557 --> 00:04:52,726
Oh, I see
that your mother is here.

105
00:04:52,726 --> 00:04:56,864
It's great to finally meet
the mother of such a good
friend of mine.

106
00:04:56,864 --> 00:05:00,701
Many is the time
you and I have engaged
in spirited high jinks.

107
00:05:00,701 --> 00:05:03,904
I enjoy your company
very, very much."

108
00:05:05,606 --> 00:05:07,174
Oh. Ahem.

109
00:05:07,174 --> 00:05:09,777
"I hope your visit from your
mother doesn't interfere

110
00:05:09,777 --> 00:05:13,847
with your brilliant science
experiments when the history
of the...

111
00:05:13,847 --> 00:05:16,249
20th century is ultimately
written,

112
00:05:16,249 --> 00:05:18,686
the name of one man
will tower above all others.

113
00:05:18,686 --> 00:05:21,889
That man is you.

114
00:05:21,889 --> 00:05:24,091
Dr. Clayton Forrester."

115
00:05:24,091 --> 00:05:27,294
"Dr. Clayton Forrester,

116
00:05:27,294 --> 00:05:30,297
you have made the world
a better place..."

117
00:05:30,297 --> 00:05:33,266
by staying underground.
I don't believe this.

118
00:05:33,266 --> 00:05:35,035
Who wrote this junk?
This is so stupid.

119
00:05:35,035 --> 00:05:37,170
"Dr. Forrester,

120
00:05:37,170 --> 00:05:40,408
perhaps no other individual
embodies the style,

121
00:05:40,408 --> 00:05:44,578
panache and verve
of the elegant sport
of polo more than you.

122
00:05:44,578 --> 00:05:47,481
You're--"
Oh, I can't do this.

123
00:05:47,481 --> 00:05:48,582
- Honey!
- This is worse--

124
00:05:48,582 --> 00:05:50,684
Your son is sick.
He's sick!

125
00:05:50,684 --> 00:05:52,553
He needs serious help.
If there's anything

126
00:05:52,553 --> 00:05:54,555
you can do to help him,
you should really--

127
00:05:54,555 --> 00:05:58,592
Well, I guess there's
really not much left

128
00:05:58,592 --> 00:06:00,227
to say other than

129
00:06:00,227 --> 00:06:03,330
your movie this week
is called "Bloodlust."

130
00:06:03,330 --> 00:06:07,801
It's immediately preceded
by a short

131
00:06:07,801 --> 00:06:10,604
called
"A Visit to Uncle Jim's Farm."

132
00:06:10,604 --> 00:06:12,573
I never visited my uncle.

133
00:06:12,573 --> 00:06:16,243
After all, a boy's best friend
is his mother.

134
00:06:17,310 --> 00:06:19,146
( both gasp )

135
00:06:19,146 --> 00:06:21,415
Now I understand
why he's so sick.

136
00:06:21,415 --> 00:06:23,651
I tempted to call her
something that rhymes
with "bitch."

137
00:06:23,651 --> 00:06:27,521
- ( alarm blaring ) 
- ( clamoring )

138
00:06:27,521 --> 00:06:30,724
Gypsy's in trouble.
Gypsy's in trouble.

139
00:06:39,833 --> 00:06:41,435
( music playing )

140
00:06:41,435 --> 00:06:43,270
- We're gonna
have to spank her.
- No, you can't say it.

141
00:06:43,270 --> 00:06:45,338
- No, you can't say it.
- Can we say it now?

142
00:06:45,338 --> 00:06:48,075
I'm Dairy Council intolerant.

143
00:06:49,610 --> 00:06:52,546
Is Velveeta a member
of the National Dairy Council?

144
00:06:52,546 --> 00:06:54,915
No, Velveeta's
a splinter group.

145
00:06:54,915 --> 00:06:57,885
I thought I smelled
something.

146
00:06:57,885 --> 00:07:01,522
Yes, the farm
of secrets and lies.

147
00:07:01,522 --> 00:07:05,425
Ladies and gentlemen,
Mrs. Loretta Doyle.

148
00:07:05,425 --> 00:07:07,628
( mutters )

149
00:07:07,628 --> 00:07:10,030
Narrator:
 This is a story

150
00:07:10,030 --> 00:07:11,532
about the summer 
 George and Betty spent

151
00:07:11,532 --> 00:07:13,366
on Uncle Jim's dairy farm.

152
00:07:13,366 --> 00:07:14,668
A cautionary tale.

153
00:07:14,668 --> 00:07:16,737
George and Betty 
 are both excited

154
00:07:16,737 --> 00:07:18,939
because this is 
 a very special trip.

155
00:07:18,939 --> 00:07:21,341
They're dropping off
the kids and never coming back.

156
00:07:21,341 --> 00:07:22,643
They're not a bit 
 more excited

157
00:07:22,643 --> 00:07:24,945
than Andy and Jane 
 who live on the farm.

158
00:07:24,945 --> 00:07:26,013
Poor saps.

159
00:07:26,013 --> 00:07:27,748
I think they're here.
I see their car.

160
00:07:27,748 --> 00:07:29,082
Are you sure?

161
00:07:29,082 --> 00:07:30,851
Don't ever question me.

162
00:07:30,851 --> 00:07:33,587
- Sure I'm sure.
- I'll run and tell Mom.

163
00:07:33,587 --> 00:07:37,290
( imitates laughter )

164
00:07:37,290 --> 00:07:40,761
Here in frame 54, Mr. Connolly
is struck by the bullet.

165
00:07:40,761 --> 00:07:42,429
No wonder 
 they're so excited.

166
00:07:42,429 --> 00:07:45,198
After Uncle Fred and Aunt Alice 
 go back to the city,

167
00:07:45,198 --> 00:07:48,602
George and Betty are going to 
 stay to spend a whole summer

168
00:07:48,602 --> 00:07:51,104
on the farm 
 with Jane and Andy.

169
00:07:51,104 --> 00:07:53,273
- Where's Uncle Jim?
- Dad's out in the field.

170
00:07:53,273 --> 00:07:54,441
Come on,
and they'll let you

171
00:07:54,441 --> 00:07:55,943
- ring the bell to call him.
- Okay.

172
00:07:55,943 --> 00:07:58,411
Let's go tip some cattle.

173
00:08:00,948 --> 00:08:02,482
Go ahead, Betty,
you ring it.

174
00:08:02,482 --> 00:08:03,551
Ah yes,
the bell signals

175
00:08:03,551 --> 00:08:07,154
a nuclear attack
in these parts.

176
00:08:07,154 --> 00:08:09,389
Now they have nothing
to look forward to.

177
00:08:09,389 --> 00:08:11,859
I never want this day
to end.

178
00:08:14,294 --> 00:08:17,297
What do you know?
Something fell in
the thresher again.

179
00:08:17,297 --> 00:08:19,933
Already the children
have disturbed Uncle Jim.

180
00:08:19,933 --> 00:08:22,936
Uncle Jim is an edgy man
who should not be riled.

181
00:08:22,936 --> 00:08:24,638
Ganja, Betty?

182
00:08:24,638 --> 00:08:26,540
The grownups have things 
 to talk about,

183
00:08:26,540 --> 00:08:29,342
but Andy and Jane have 
 their plans all set.

184
00:08:29,342 --> 00:08:32,379
The first thing to do 
 is show their city cousins

185
00:08:32,379 --> 00:08:33,881
some new 
 things around the farm.

186
00:08:33,881 --> 00:08:36,049
Their throats tightened
with apprehension.

187
00:08:36,049 --> 00:08:37,317
Because it is 
 a dairy farm...

188
00:08:37,317 --> 00:08:38,686
Oh, Bossie--

189
00:08:38,686 --> 00:08:41,154
Oh, nothing,
I'm not doing anything.

190
00:08:41,154 --> 00:08:44,257
Where their older brother Bill 
 has started the milking.

191
00:08:44,257 --> 00:08:46,159
- How are you?
- We're fine.

192
00:08:46,159 --> 00:08:49,396
- My, it's clean in here.
- That's right, Betty.

193
00:08:49,396 --> 00:08:53,133
All dairy farms
wanna keep their barns
clean and their cows, too.

194
00:08:53,133 --> 00:08:55,335
Is that why you're
washing the cow?

195
00:08:55,335 --> 00:08:58,205
Every morning and evening,
George, just before
they're milked.

196
00:08:58,205 --> 00:09:00,007
This is one way we help
to make sure the milk

197
00:09:00,007 --> 00:09:01,709
is always
pure and wholesome

198
00:09:01,709 --> 00:09:03,010
and good tasting, too.

199
00:09:03,010 --> 00:09:04,011
Go on, taste her.

200
00:09:04,011 --> 00:09:05,879
See those pipes up there?

201
00:09:05,879 --> 00:09:07,781
The milk runs from the cow
through those pipes

202
00:09:07,781 --> 00:09:11,852
through the big cooler
in the milk room.

203
00:09:11,852 --> 00:09:13,153
Yeah, so it keeps
cold and fresh

204
00:09:13,153 --> 00:09:16,223
Excuse me, back here.
I need some help back here.

205
00:09:16,223 --> 00:09:17,891
I got something caught
in my--

206
00:09:17,891 --> 00:09:20,227
Uncle Jim,
how do cows make milk?

207
00:09:20,227 --> 00:09:22,562
They make it out
of what they eat.

208
00:09:22,562 --> 00:09:24,497
Yeah, but what
do they eat?

209
00:09:24,497 --> 00:09:26,499
Come on, I'll show you.

210
00:09:26,499 --> 00:09:30,137
The kids feign
interest so as not
to rile Uncle Jim.

211
00:09:30,137 --> 00:09:33,874
See, we dump
this stuff in the creek

212
00:09:33,874 --> 00:09:35,508
and the government
pays us for it.

213
00:09:35,508 --> 00:09:37,945
Wow, look at it
all come out.

214
00:09:37,945 --> 00:09:41,281
I just saw a finger.

215
00:09:41,281 --> 00:09:45,152
Can we go home?
( crying )

216
00:09:45,152 --> 00:09:47,621
This is what the cows
eat at milking time.

217
00:09:47,621 --> 00:09:49,757
Pure, uncut.

218
00:09:49,757 --> 00:09:51,358
First one's free, kid.

219
00:09:51,358 --> 00:09:53,694
Why, that looks like some
of our breakfast cereal.

220
00:09:53,694 --> 00:09:55,062
( mumbles mockingly )

221
00:09:55,062 --> 00:09:56,930
Well, that's pretty much
what it is, Betty.

222
00:09:56,930 --> 00:09:59,566
As a matter of fact,
cows are a lot like people.

223
00:09:59,566 --> 00:10:00,868
They're sinners.

224
00:10:00,868 --> 00:10:02,770
This feed is a special mixture
of ground grain

225
00:10:02,770 --> 00:10:04,371
with the extra vitamins
and minerals

226
00:10:04,371 --> 00:10:06,473
the cows must eat
to stay healthy.

227
00:10:06,473 --> 00:10:09,743
Of course they eat grass
and hay and silage, too.

228
00:10:09,743 --> 00:10:12,379
They eat all different kinds
of good food.

229
00:10:12,379 --> 00:10:16,149
Plenty of good, clean water
and a clean, comfortable
place to live.

230
00:10:16,149 --> 00:10:18,551
That way they grow
to be strong and healthy.

231
00:10:18,551 --> 00:10:19,853
When we slaughter them.

232
00:10:19,853 --> 00:10:21,354
...help your brother
Bill with the milking.

233
00:10:21,354 --> 00:10:24,658
Andy, I think that Betty
and George would like
to see the hayloft.

234
00:10:24,658 --> 00:10:27,728
- What do you think?
- ( cheering )

235
00:10:27,728 --> 00:10:29,897
Not really.
Is there anything on TV?

236
00:10:29,897 --> 00:10:32,766
Now Junior Samples
and Lulu Belle make
their appearance.

237
00:10:33,834 --> 00:10:35,602
( playful music plays )

238
00:10:35,602 --> 00:10:39,539
( imitates music )

239
00:10:41,241 --> 00:10:42,876
Oh, my.
I don't think--
'cause my doctor--

240
00:10:42,876 --> 00:10:44,211
I'm frail
and I have allergies,

241
00:10:44,211 --> 00:10:45,545
but if you
really want me to.

242
00:10:45,545 --> 00:10:48,648
- ( laughing )
- I found a pitchfork.

243
00:10:52,285 --> 00:10:54,855
All the commotion
provokes a bull snake
resting in the hay.

244
00:10:56,656 --> 00:11:00,493
I gotta be careful of my new
jeans 'cause I got them
at Pamida.

245
00:11:02,562 --> 00:11:05,933
Tonight on ESPN 2,
hayloft rope swinging.

246
00:11:05,933 --> 00:11:08,335
You'd better watch
'cause it might happen.

247
00:11:08,335 --> 00:11:12,973
This is when you get that
tingly feeling.

248
00:11:12,973 --> 00:11:15,275
It's easier when
a stagehand helps.

249
00:11:15,275 --> 00:11:18,912
( cheering )

250
00:11:18,912 --> 00:11:20,814
( humming )

251
00:11:22,983 --> 00:11:25,318
The summer of my
German humiliation.

252
00:11:25,318 --> 00:11:27,354
- I can't do it.
- Come on!

253
00:11:27,354 --> 00:11:28,521
( shouting )

254
00:11:28,521 --> 00:11:32,492
But I wanna write
poetry and act and um...

255
00:11:32,492 --> 00:11:33,994
My turn!

256
00:11:33,994 --> 00:11:35,796
I can't do it.

257
00:11:35,796 --> 00:11:37,397
That's okay, George.

258
00:11:37,397 --> 00:11:39,867
You can't do it
now, but the bigger
and stronger you get

259
00:11:39,867 --> 00:11:42,335
and the more you practice,
the more things you can do.

260
00:11:42,335 --> 00:11:44,071
You'll be able to do it
by the end of the summer.

261
00:11:44,071 --> 00:11:47,674
Now give me 30, soldier.

262
00:11:47,674 --> 00:11:49,509
George and Betty 
 soon discovered

263
00:11:49,509 --> 00:11:52,079
that on a farm everybody 
 has work to do.

264
00:11:52,079 --> 00:11:53,713
This Guernsey
does the typing.

265
00:11:53,713 --> 00:11:56,516
Right now this new calf 
 doesn't need any help.

266
00:11:56,516 --> 00:12:00,020
It's just two days old, 
 but it knows where 
 its dinner is.

267
00:12:00,020 --> 00:12:02,155
Hey, hey, hey, no snacking.

268
00:12:02,155 --> 00:12:03,991
It will be Andy's job 
 to teach the calf

269
00:12:03,991 --> 00:12:06,860
to drink its milk from a pail 
 and to eat other feed.

270
00:12:06,860 --> 00:12:08,862
Hey, this is skim.

271
00:12:08,862 --> 00:12:10,063
Uncle Jim and Bill 
 make sure

272
00:12:10,063 --> 00:12:12,766
the grownup cows 
 are properly fed.

273
00:12:12,766 --> 00:12:15,869
That they get grain 
 and grass and silage.

274
00:12:15,869 --> 00:12:17,270
All the good food 
 and clean water

275
00:12:17,270 --> 00:12:20,173
they need to make 
 good fresh milk.

276
00:12:20,173 --> 00:12:22,275
You'll pay one day, hayseed.

277
00:12:22,275 --> 00:12:24,244
You'll get what's coming,
farmer.

278
00:12:24,244 --> 00:12:26,413
There was plenty 
 for everybody to do.

279
00:12:26,413 --> 00:12:29,316
Betty helped Jane 
 take care of the baby lamb.

280
00:12:29,316 --> 00:12:31,318
- And prepare a mint sauce.
- ( slurping )

281
00:12:31,318 --> 00:12:34,021
And they all learned 
 about pigs, too.

282
00:12:34,021 --> 00:12:35,622
More than you'd ever
wanna know.

283
00:12:35,622 --> 00:12:37,324
( shouting )

284
00:12:37,324 --> 00:12:40,160
Don't ever do that.

285
00:12:40,160 --> 00:12:42,362
( squealing )

286
00:12:42,362 --> 00:12:46,233
A very special Ziffel
family reunion.

287
00:12:47,767 --> 00:12:50,370
So this is enough to get
you up in the morning, huh?

288
00:12:50,370 --> 00:12:54,842
Okay, so cities, night clubs
don't mean anything to you,
do they?

289
00:12:54,842 --> 00:12:57,377
George and Andy helped Bill 
 feed the pigs every day.

290
00:12:57,377 --> 00:12:59,379
Day after life-sucking day.

291
00:12:59,379 --> 00:13:01,514
While Betty and Jane helped 
 with the babies.

292
00:13:01,514 --> 00:13:03,917
Drink it, just drink it.
( chokes )

293
00:13:03,917 --> 00:13:06,153
These would make nice purses.

294
00:13:08,488 --> 00:13:11,491
Oh, the lunch counter
at Tom and Rosie's diner.

295
00:13:11,491 --> 00:13:14,061
They don't have good 
 table manners, but they 
 enjoy their dinner,

296
00:13:14,061 --> 00:13:16,830
a mixture of milk 
 and ground grain.

297
00:13:16,830 --> 00:13:19,332
Later they will eat corn 
 and other kinds of feed.

298
00:13:19,332 --> 00:13:21,835
Yeah, and then later,
we'll bring around
the dessert tray.

299
00:13:21,835 --> 00:13:23,403
Baby chicks, too...

300
00:13:23,403 --> 00:13:25,805
Hey, they're women.
Oh. I see.

301
00:13:25,805 --> 00:13:28,575
A special mixture 
 of ground up grain, 
 milk and other feed.

302
00:13:28,575 --> 00:13:31,912
This is Betty's 
 and Jane's favorite chore.

303
00:13:31,912 --> 00:13:34,481
The baby chicks 
 are so soft and cuddly.

304
00:13:34,481 --> 00:13:36,283
And squishable.

305
00:13:36,283 --> 00:13:38,185
On the farm, 
 a big dog

306
00:13:38,185 --> 00:13:40,287
has plenty of room 
 to run and play.

307
00:13:40,287 --> 00:13:43,891
But he is more than a pet. 
 He also protects the family.

308
00:13:43,891 --> 00:13:45,758
From rival farm gangs.

309
00:13:45,758 --> 00:13:47,027
But all the puppies 
 have to do

310
00:13:47,027 --> 00:13:48,361
is eat 
 and play and sleep...

311
00:13:48,361 --> 00:13:49,462
Oh, puppy.
Oh, look at the puppy.

312
00:13:49,462 --> 00:13:51,031
( speaking gibberish )

313
00:13:51,031 --> 00:13:54,767
* On a foggy Christmas Eve,
Santa came to say *

314
00:13:54,767 --> 00:13:56,769
Jane's kitten loves 
 to drink his milk.

315
00:13:56,769 --> 00:13:59,206
But he wishes
he had a straw.

316
00:13:59,206 --> 00:14:01,909
And Jane and Betty enjoy 
 playing with the kittens.

317
00:14:01,909 --> 00:14:03,877
They enjoy neutering
them also.

318
00:14:03,877 --> 00:14:05,778
( bell dings )

319
00:14:05,778 --> 00:14:09,249
When the dinner bell rings, 
 no one has to call 
 the girls twice.

320
00:14:09,249 --> 00:14:13,420
They run to the house 
 and wash up quickly so 
 they won't be late.

321
00:14:13,420 --> 00:14:15,422
They have obsessive-compulsive
behavior.

322
00:14:15,422 --> 00:14:19,927
Living on a farm makes you 
 hungry and Aunt Helen is 
 certainly a good cook.

323
00:14:19,927 --> 00:14:21,594
Given her limitations.

324
00:14:25,132 --> 00:14:28,068
Jerry Lee Lewis joins
the family for dinner.

325
00:14:30,837 --> 00:14:33,673
- That's nice.
- Ah, yes, now's the time

326
00:14:33,673 --> 00:14:36,376
for Uncle Jim's
fundamentalist dogma.

327
00:14:36,376 --> 00:14:38,545
But I don't like food.

328
00:14:38,545 --> 00:14:40,847
Look at all the wonderful 
 things to eat.

329
00:14:40,847 --> 00:14:43,083
At restaurants.

330
00:14:43,083 --> 00:14:46,253
It takes all kinds of good food 
 to build up muscles

331
00:14:46,253 --> 00:14:48,155
and let us grow bigger 
 and stronger.

332
00:14:48,155 --> 00:14:51,458
Mostly just butter.

333
00:14:51,458 --> 00:14:54,461
Susie drains the last
of her Dirty Banana.
( slurps )

334
00:14:54,461 --> 00:14:56,196
That's why I'm burning my bra.

335
00:14:58,598 --> 00:15:02,502
Repressed emotions
help the evening pass
without incident.

336
00:15:06,739 --> 00:15:08,908
Man, your dad goes on.

337
00:15:08,908 --> 00:15:10,210
The children 
 are ready for bed

338
00:15:10,210 --> 00:15:12,479
after a busy day 
 on the farm.

339
00:15:12,479 --> 00:15:15,615
Day one is over
at long last.

340
00:15:17,784 --> 00:15:19,987
She's dreaming
of midtown Manhattan.

341
00:15:19,987 --> 00:15:21,955
( laughs )

342
00:15:21,955 --> 00:15:23,156
( imitates snoring )

343
00:15:23,156 --> 00:15:24,424
( rooster crows )

344
00:15:24,424 --> 00:15:26,626
Three hours later,
it's up again.

345
00:15:26,626 --> 00:15:28,895
Back home a Hershey's
truck is overturned

346
00:15:28,895 --> 00:15:31,364
and everyone is getting
all the free chocolate
they want.

347
00:15:33,700 --> 00:15:36,269
Uncle Jim's out of control.
We frag him today.

348
00:15:36,269 --> 00:15:39,072
This is the way 
 the summer went.

349
00:15:39,072 --> 00:15:43,110
Play, work and rest. 
 The children helped 
 with the chores.

350
00:15:43,110 --> 00:15:44,544
Let my people go!

351
00:15:44,544 --> 00:15:46,179
While Bill helped 
 in the corn field.

352
00:15:46,179 --> 00:15:48,281
Despair was
the bumper crop.

353
00:15:48,281 --> 00:15:51,318
Andy and George kept 
 the vegetable garden 
 clear of weeds.

354
00:15:51,318 --> 00:15:54,521
I remember ringing a bell.

355
00:15:54,521 --> 00:15:57,924
Jane and Betty brought in 
 fresh eggs each day.

356
00:15:57,924 --> 00:15:59,259
Given by
disgruntled chickens.

357
00:15:59,259 --> 00:16:01,194
And helped Aunt Helen 
 with the housework.

358
00:16:01,194 --> 00:16:04,364
I was Rhubarb Queen
of the five state area in '48.

359
00:16:04,364 --> 00:16:08,601
So many gentlemen callers
I had in my day.

360
00:16:08,601 --> 00:16:11,538
It was fun, too, 
 especially riding the horses.

361
00:16:11,538 --> 00:16:14,874
Why, it's a wonder
cities even exist.

362
00:16:14,874 --> 00:16:17,710
Whenever they were hungry 
 in the middle of the afternoon,

363
00:16:17,710 --> 00:16:19,046
Too bad.

364
00:16:19,046 --> 00:16:20,780
there were snacks 
 under the shady trees...

365
00:16:20,780 --> 00:16:23,716
They had to graze
just like the animals.

366
00:16:26,019 --> 00:16:29,689
Mmm, pigs' knuckles.

367
00:16:29,689 --> 00:16:31,991
Uncle Jim's sick again.

368
00:16:31,991 --> 00:16:33,960
Mmm. Good white,
hearty white bread

369
00:16:33,960 --> 00:16:37,064
for white, white people.

370
00:16:37,064 --> 00:16:41,234
Sis takes to the comfort
of cream items.

371
00:16:41,234 --> 00:16:43,470
And twice a week, 
 letters from home

372
00:16:43,470 --> 00:16:45,772
in the big mailbox 
 beside the highway.

373
00:16:45,772 --> 00:16:48,141
Thank you for your request
to come pick you up.

374
00:16:48,141 --> 00:16:50,610
We are sorry to inform
you that we cannot.

375
00:16:50,610 --> 00:16:53,513
As the summer passed, 
 everything seemed to grow.

376
00:16:53,513 --> 00:16:57,917
The corn was knee high 
 on George when he came. 
 Now it's taller than he is.

377
00:16:57,917 --> 00:16:59,686
George is clearly shrinking.

378
00:16:59,686 --> 00:17:03,123
Andy's calf is bigger 
 and eating like a grown cow.

379
00:17:03,123 --> 00:17:05,392
With a knife and fork
like a real gentleman.

380
00:17:05,392 --> 00:17:08,095
The lambs 
 are almost grown.

381
00:17:08,095 --> 00:17:10,397
And look how big 
 the pigs are now.

382
00:17:10,397 --> 00:17:12,199
Equal rights for pigs.

383
00:17:12,199 --> 00:17:14,601
The little chicks 
 have become grown chicks.

384
00:17:14,601 --> 00:17:17,237
They want to wear
makeup and nylons.

385
00:17:17,237 --> 00:17:19,372
George and Betty have grown 
 bigger and stronger, too.

386
00:17:19,372 --> 00:17:21,941
George can even shinny up 
 that rope now.

387
00:17:21,941 --> 00:17:23,410
The stagehand
helps him, too.

388
00:17:23,410 --> 00:17:26,045
Yes, they've had 
 a wonderful time.

389
00:17:26,045 --> 00:17:27,514
They've learned 
 how animals grow

390
00:17:27,514 --> 00:17:29,182
through good, 
 nourishing food.

391
00:17:29,182 --> 00:17:31,184
I think it's dead.

392
00:17:31,184 --> 00:17:34,921
They've learned how work 
 and play and fun go 
 together on the farm.

393
00:17:34,921 --> 00:17:37,090
Elsie and I are in love
and you can't stop us.

394
00:17:37,090 --> 00:17:41,128
They've had good, 
 nourishing food to grow on

395
00:17:41,128 --> 00:17:42,729
and plenty of sleep.

396
00:17:42,729 --> 00:17:45,965
Why, Uncle Jim's
even enrolled them
in the posse comitatus.

397
00:17:45,965 --> 00:17:50,903
Corn coming after me.
Stalks attacking me.
Oh! Oh!

398
00:17:50,903 --> 00:17:54,341
The summer is over 
 and George and Betty 
 are waiting for their parents

399
00:17:54,341 --> 00:17:55,942
to take them back 
 to the city

400
00:17:55,942 --> 00:17:58,578
- Freedom!
- Freedom!

401
00:17:58,578 --> 00:18:01,748
Abner, just throw them
their winter clothes
and then floor it.

402
00:18:01,748 --> 00:18:06,386
Hey, they're driving
right by. Wait, wait.

403
00:18:06,386 --> 00:18:08,355
It's time 
 for goodbye now,

404
00:18:08,355 --> 00:18:09,722
but the children 
 will have a lot

405
00:18:09,722 --> 00:18:11,324
to tell their friends 
 at home.

406
00:18:11,324 --> 00:18:13,126
Heck, I was pretty
hard on 'em, Clark,

407
00:18:13,126 --> 00:18:17,664
but I think you'll
understand when you see
how well behaved they are.

408
00:18:17,664 --> 00:18:21,100
It'll be a long time 
 before either George 
 and Betty...

409
00:18:21,100 --> 00:18:22,435
Learn to forgive.

410
00:18:22,435 --> 00:18:25,104
...or Andy and Jane forget 
 that summer of fun

411
00:18:25,104 --> 00:18:27,374
on Uncle Jim's 
 dairy farm.

412
00:18:27,374 --> 00:18:29,108
You weren't there when we
had our summer of fun.

413
00:18:29,108 --> 00:18:31,811
We went to a condo
in Orlando.

414
00:18:33,546 --> 00:18:37,116
Goodbye. We'll send you
that arm if we find it.

415
00:18:38,218 --> 00:18:41,488
So long,
you corn-shucking suckers.

416
00:18:41,488 --> 00:18:43,523
( imitates car engine )

417
00:18:43,523 --> 00:18:47,160
I'm Jam Handy. ( laughs )

418
00:18:47,160 --> 00:18:49,095
**

419
00:18:53,065 --> 00:18:54,634
( eerie music playing )

420
00:18:54,634 --> 00:18:59,206
- Ooh. The story
of Wisconsin sausage.
- ( laughs )

421
00:18:59,206 --> 00:19:03,376
You get your hand out of
that cookie jar, young man.

422
00:19:03,376 --> 00:19:08,181
He's so lucky.
He has those high cheekbones.

423
00:19:08,181 --> 00:19:12,652
That's the Edvard Munch
painting that wasn't stolen.

424
00:19:12,652 --> 00:19:16,489
Looks like Ivan Lendl.

425
00:19:16,489 --> 00:19:19,926
Nope, sorry, can't work up
any interest in that group
of people.

426
00:19:19,926 --> 00:19:21,694
Right.

427
00:19:24,497 --> 00:19:27,099
This isn't a very good
animation sequence.

428
00:19:29,336 --> 00:19:32,405
This is exactly how
Leakey found Lucy.

429
00:19:32,405 --> 00:19:35,375
She was leaky.

430
00:19:35,375 --> 00:19:40,046
This is the best Saul Bass
credit sequence ever.

431
00:19:40,046 --> 00:19:43,316
You know, can't say these
people interest me, either.

432
00:19:43,316 --> 00:19:44,951
Nope, not a lick.

433
00:19:44,951 --> 00:19:47,654
- Well, how about--
- No.

434
00:19:47,654 --> 00:19:49,789
No.

435
00:19:49,789 --> 00:19:52,091
Come on, make
with the fright already.

436
00:19:52,091 --> 00:19:55,862
Yeah, bring
on the scare, man.

437
00:19:55,862 --> 00:19:59,232
Cun-- the big Cunha.
Cunha.

438
00:19:59,232 --> 00:20:00,833
- Cunha.
- Cunha

439
00:20:00,833 --> 00:20:05,272
Oh, yes, it's Ralph
"It's my picture,
we do it my way" Brooke.

440
00:20:06,973 --> 00:20:10,343
All right, Brooke, Mr. Auteur,
let's see what you got.

441
00:20:10,343 --> 00:20:12,512
Bring back
the skeleton skull guy.

442
00:20:12,512 --> 00:20:16,649
They are so out
in the middle of the sea.

443
00:20:16,649 --> 00:20:18,951
Fellas, it's too rough
to feed you.

444
00:20:18,951 --> 00:20:20,086
Tony, watch it, will ya?

445
00:20:20,086 --> 00:20:22,221
We wanna get back
to port nice and dry.

446
00:20:22,221 --> 00:20:24,557
Yeah, you trying to drown
us or something?

447
00:20:24,557 --> 00:20:27,594
Don't worry.
Just don't worry.

448
00:20:27,594 --> 00:20:28,628
The Exxon Valdez.

449
00:20:28,628 --> 00:20:30,162
...get home
safe and sound.

450
00:20:30,162 --> 00:20:32,532
I can navigate
this with one hand.

451
00:20:32,532 --> 00:20:36,135
Uh-oh, Johnny,
I think he's at it again.

452
00:20:36,135 --> 00:20:38,237
Yes, it's the booze barge.

453
00:20:38,237 --> 00:20:41,408
* You see, Brandy,
you're a fine girl. *

454
00:20:41,408 --> 00:20:42,709
( slurring )
Stay right there.

455
00:20:42,709 --> 00:20:44,377
I wanna know where
you are at all times.

456
00:20:44,377 --> 00:20:48,147
If he does that once more,
I think I'm gonna get sick.

457
00:20:48,147 --> 00:20:50,317
I think all this action
has made the fish sick.

458
00:20:50,317 --> 00:20:52,285
I can't seem to make any
contact with them at all.

459
00:20:52,285 --> 00:20:53,653
Shut up, four eyes.

460
00:20:53,653 --> 00:20:54,987
Okay, Betty,
let her go.

461
00:20:54,987 --> 00:20:56,789
She's tossing her panties.

462
00:20:56,789 --> 00:20:58,190
( gunshot )

463
00:20:58,190 --> 00:20:59,392
That's the last pair, honey.

464
00:20:59,392 --> 00:21:02,194
Hey, not bad.
Not bad at all.

465
00:21:02,194 --> 00:21:03,296
Here, you wanna try it?

466
00:21:03,296 --> 00:21:06,433
Sure. Only there's one hitch.

467
00:21:06,433 --> 00:21:08,267
- What?
- No more bottles.

468
00:21:08,267 --> 00:21:11,237
The captain
can rectify that.

469
00:21:11,237 --> 00:21:13,105
Guess we'll have to play
a different game.

470
00:21:13,105 --> 00:21:14,741
Chutes and Ladders.

471
00:21:14,741 --> 00:21:15,908
What else can
we do out here?

472
00:21:15,908 --> 00:21:18,978
This is always fun.

473
00:21:18,978 --> 00:21:20,980
Looking at your roots.

474
00:21:24,517 --> 00:21:25,885
Oh, honey, I think
you really hurt

475
00:21:25,885 --> 00:21:27,720
something this time.

476
00:21:27,720 --> 00:21:29,322
Of all the girls
in the world,

477
00:21:29,322 --> 00:21:31,591
I had to get mixed up
with the daughter
of a judo expert.

478
00:21:31,591 --> 00:21:32,925
Foreshadowing.

479
00:21:32,925 --> 00:21:35,862
Daddy told me lots
of men like to wrestle.

480
00:21:35,862 --> 00:21:37,764
That's not exactly
what I had in mind.

481
00:21:37,764 --> 00:21:41,100
I want you to smash my face
into the side of the ship.

482
00:21:42,935 --> 00:21:45,071
I'm glad I bought
my Bolero jacket.

483
00:21:45,071 --> 00:21:47,306
How come you don't judo me?

484
00:21:47,306 --> 00:21:49,976
- Come here.
- What is it?

485
00:21:49,976 --> 00:21:52,345
Water. Lots of it.

486
00:21:52,345 --> 00:21:53,346
There's an island
way over there

487
00:21:53,346 --> 00:21:55,247
that I don't remember
seeing before.

488
00:21:55,247 --> 00:21:58,385
Yeah, large land masses
are easy to miss.

489
00:21:58,385 --> 00:22:01,120
This is the brightest day
we've had on our vacation.

490
00:22:01,120 --> 00:22:03,390
Must have been hidden
in the mists out there before.

491
00:22:03,390 --> 00:22:07,360
A mysterious hidden island.
Let's go ashore.

492
00:22:07,360 --> 00:22:08,361
( mocking )
Let's go ashore.

493
00:22:08,361 --> 00:22:09,729
What for?

494
00:22:09,729 --> 00:22:12,765
Well, maybe we can find
some hidden pirate treasure.

495
00:22:12,765 --> 00:22:14,033
In the gift shop.

496
00:22:14,033 --> 00:22:17,003
It would be fun.
What do you say, Johnny?

497
00:22:17,003 --> 00:22:18,671
Why not? This boat's
costing us enough.

498
00:22:18,671 --> 00:22:20,607
We might as well
see everything we can.

499
00:22:20,607 --> 00:22:24,010
Hey, Tony, pull in here.
We wanna go ashore.

500
00:22:24,010 --> 00:22:27,046
- ( laughing )
- Can do.

501
00:22:27,046 --> 00:22:29,416
Robert Maxwell,
the untold story.

502
00:22:31,183 --> 00:22:35,655
I'll just run up to
the Lido Deck and see
what Isaac is doing.

503
00:22:35,655 --> 00:22:38,691
Well, he's dead.
Why didn't he just say so?

504
00:22:38,691 --> 00:22:40,860
Boy, he's really tied
one on this time.

505
00:22:40,860 --> 00:22:43,162
Looks like he's gonna
be out for quite a while.

506
00:22:43,162 --> 00:22:44,697
Yeah.

507
00:22:44,697 --> 00:22:45,698
I know I can get
this boat in

508
00:22:45,698 --> 00:22:46,699
close enough
to the island

509
00:22:46,699 --> 00:22:48,134
to get the anchor down.

510
00:22:48,134 --> 00:22:51,370
But I'm not sure I can
get it back to the main island.

511
00:22:51,370 --> 00:22:52,872
So I guess
our little treasure hunt

512
00:22:52,872 --> 00:22:55,608
is the best
thing to do till
our navigator sobers up.

513
00:22:55,608 --> 00:22:57,544
I'll just keeping making
wry comments.

514
00:22:57,544 --> 00:22:58,845
...get the dinghy ready,
I'll take us in closer
to the shore.

515
00:22:58,845 --> 00:22:59,846
Okay.

516
00:22:59,846 --> 00:23:01,347
What's a dinghy?

517
00:23:01,347 --> 00:23:02,582
Thank you.

518
00:23:02,582 --> 00:23:04,817
Please be sure to fill
out your comment cards.

519
00:23:06,553 --> 00:23:10,122
Sure hope you know
how to fly this thing.

520
00:23:10,122 --> 00:23:13,292
Okay, we detonate it
about 100 yards out.

521
00:23:13,292 --> 00:23:17,730
Oh, man, I went home
with this boat last night?

522
00:23:17,730 --> 00:23:20,032
Ugh, time to visit
the poop deck,

523
00:23:20,032 --> 00:23:21,601
if you know
what I mean.

524
00:23:24,236 --> 00:23:26,539
Wait, you forgot
Tallulah Bankhead.

525
00:23:26,539 --> 00:23:30,376
Should we have
brought oars or not?

526
00:23:30,376 --> 00:23:32,579
I could quit anytime.

527
00:23:32,579 --> 00:23:36,048
Come back.
Come back, you fools!

528
00:23:36,048 --> 00:23:39,852
Don't land there.
Come back.

529
00:23:39,852 --> 00:23:42,755
The cruise
of wine and roses.

530
00:23:42,755 --> 00:23:47,393
All right, back to work.
( snoring )

531
00:23:47,393 --> 00:23:49,596
Oh, no,
they forgot the piano.

532
00:23:51,531 --> 00:23:55,401
Tonight, "Love
and the Landing at Normandy."

533
00:23:55,401 --> 00:23:58,137
Hey, I wonder if
anyone lives here.

534
00:23:58,137 --> 00:24:00,540
No chance.
If there had been
any natives on this island,

535
00:24:00,540 --> 00:24:02,241
they'd have spotted
the boat before now.

536
00:24:02,241 --> 00:24:03,910
They'd be down here trying
to sell us hand-painted
coconuts.

537
00:24:03,910 --> 00:24:05,645
What do we do now?

538
00:24:05,645 --> 00:24:07,847
Well, my intuition
tells me

539
00:24:07,847 --> 00:24:09,649
that Captain Kidd buried
his loot over there.

540
00:24:09,649 --> 00:24:11,183
In her bra?

541
00:24:11,183 --> 00:24:13,720
Let's race up there.
Last one doesn't get
any of his jewels.

542
00:24:13,720 --> 00:24:15,955
( laughing )

543
00:24:15,955 --> 00:24:19,025
Hey, counter worker
from Long John Silver's.

544
00:24:19,025 --> 00:24:20,527
Go fish.

545
00:24:20,527 --> 00:24:25,297
Shoot, I wanted to sell
them painted coconuts.

546
00:24:25,297 --> 00:24:26,799
Oh!

547
00:24:26,799 --> 00:24:28,300
See, what did I tell you?

548
00:24:28,300 --> 00:24:30,870
This looks
just like the remains
of Captain Morgan's flagship.

549
00:24:30,870 --> 00:24:33,105
Back there you said
it was Captain Kidd.

550
00:24:33,105 --> 00:24:34,206
I did?

551
00:24:34,206 --> 00:24:35,642
So you're saying
I'm not good in bed?

552
00:24:35,642 --> 00:24:37,476
One pirate's gold
is as good as another.

553
00:24:37,476 --> 00:24:40,613
Backpedaling.

554
00:24:40,613 --> 00:24:41,881
I think
I found something.

555
00:24:41,881 --> 00:24:44,350
Oh, a hand, sorry.

556
00:24:44,350 --> 00:24:48,187
What is it?
Armor? Pirate's armor?

557
00:24:48,187 --> 00:24:51,423
Well, let's just say it's
something the tide washed in.

558
00:24:51,423 --> 00:24:53,259
Remember, I know more
than you do.

559
00:24:53,259 --> 00:24:56,162
I think you're just a little
too young to know about it.

560
00:24:56,162 --> 00:24:58,030
Yeah, women no nothing
about underwear.

561
00:24:58,030 --> 00:24:59,131
Hey, gang, come here.

562
00:24:59,131 --> 00:25:00,900
I'm Robert Reed.

563
00:25:04,036 --> 00:25:06,639
Found some turtle eggs.
Let's smash 'em.

564
00:25:06,639 --> 00:25:08,908
It's a head, I think.

565
00:25:08,908 --> 00:25:10,577
- Did you find something?
- You bet I did. Look.

566
00:25:10,577 --> 00:25:12,511
Linguini.

567
00:25:12,511 --> 00:25:14,413
It's some of the best
eating in these waters.

568
00:25:14,413 --> 00:25:16,282
A lot of clams
around here, too.

569
00:25:16,282 --> 00:25:19,251
Getting hungry.
Let's have a clambake.

570
00:25:19,251 --> 00:25:22,589
- Right. Let's have it
tropical style
- So how do you mean?

571
00:25:22,589 --> 00:25:23,590
Pete and I will go
into the jungle

572
00:25:23,590 --> 00:25:24,591
and get some
banana leaves...

573
00:25:24,591 --> 00:25:26,993
In back,
it's a Japanese sub.

574
00:25:26,993 --> 00:25:30,329
Hey, a Japanese sub.

575
00:25:30,329 --> 00:25:33,465
...except the last part.
Where you go, we go.

576
00:25:33,465 --> 00:25:34,934
- Right?
- Right.

577
00:25:34,934 --> 00:25:37,570
We'll all go for the leaves
and then we'll all dig.

578
00:25:37,570 --> 00:25:40,439
Okay, okay.
Come on, let's go.

579
00:25:40,439 --> 00:25:43,843
Unfortunately, they landed
on Bikini Atoll in 1947.

580
00:25:43,843 --> 00:25:45,511
( makes explosion sounds )

581
00:25:45,511 --> 00:25:48,414
The lure of the clambake
proved to be their undoing.

582
00:25:48,414 --> 00:25:51,651
Great plan.
Can we just go
to Red Lobster?

583
00:25:51,651 --> 00:25:53,085
What is a jungle
without banana trees?

584
00:25:53,085 --> 00:25:54,553
Uh, a forest?

585
00:25:54,553 --> 00:25:56,623
Hey, wait a minute.

586
00:25:56,623 --> 00:25:59,592
If you lived here,
you'd be home-- what?

587
00:25:59,592 --> 00:26:00,593
It's not too old,
either.

588
00:26:00,593 --> 00:26:01,928
I can still
smell the tobacco.

589
00:26:01,928 --> 00:26:04,030
That means somebody
does live here.

590
00:26:04,030 --> 00:26:05,464
Not necessarily.
Maybe somebody

591
00:26:05,464 --> 00:26:06,666
just went exploring
a couple days ago

592
00:26:06,666 --> 00:26:08,567
and then sailed away.

593
00:26:08,567 --> 00:26:09,969
I don't know why,
but I'm scared.

594
00:26:09,969 --> 00:26:11,503
Let's go back
to the boat.

595
00:26:11,503 --> 00:26:13,873
There's nothing
to be scared of.

596
00:26:13,873 --> 00:26:15,174
We'll only take
a few minutes.

597
00:26:15,174 --> 00:26:17,610
Then we'll be back
at the beach, come on.

598
00:26:17,610 --> 00:26:20,546
It's a few minutes
and we're not back
at the beach.

599
00:26:22,615 --> 00:26:24,283
On his resume,
Robert Reed

600
00:26:24,283 --> 00:26:26,185
listed this
as "The Tempest."

601
00:26:27,820 --> 00:26:30,222
She's a Breck girl.

602
00:26:32,391 --> 00:26:34,661
Sounds like James Galway's
bringing up the rear.

603
00:26:34,661 --> 00:26:36,228
- Whoa!
- Johnny!

604
00:26:36,228 --> 00:26:39,231
How long
do we bake the clams?

605
00:26:39,231 --> 00:26:41,834
( screams )
Johnny!

606
00:26:41,834 --> 00:26:43,435
Just bury him.

607
00:26:43,435 --> 00:26:44,771
We've got to get him
out of there.
He looks hurt.

608
00:26:44,771 --> 00:26:47,339
I'll get some vines.
We can use them
to pull him out.

609
00:26:47,339 --> 00:26:49,141
Or hang myself.

610
00:26:49,141 --> 00:26:51,978
- It was deliberately dug here.
- But who could have done it?

611
00:26:51,978 --> 00:26:53,746
Oh, I don't know.

612
00:26:53,746 --> 00:26:55,081
But I do know
that as soon as we get him

613
00:26:55,081 --> 00:26:57,216
up out of that hole,
we're getting out of here.

614
00:26:57,216 --> 00:26:59,652
Wait a minute, Pete.
I'll climb down.

615
00:26:59,652 --> 00:27:01,053
We'll need you here
to pull him up.

616
00:27:01,053 --> 00:27:03,622
Yeah, we'll need your
massive bulk up here.

617
00:27:07,093 --> 00:27:09,061
A little peek
into their home life.

618
00:27:09,061 --> 00:27:12,531
- They should just clambake him.
- Yeah.

619
00:27:12,531 --> 00:27:15,968
Okay, start pulling.

620
00:27:15,968 --> 00:27:18,037
Speaking of pulling.

621
00:27:18,037 --> 00:27:21,841
I'm here to tell you
about Mutual of Omaha.

622
00:27:24,210 --> 00:27:27,146
Cuff them quickly.

623
00:27:41,193 --> 00:27:43,062
Well, seems
like a good idea

624
00:27:43,062 --> 00:27:46,032
and hey, good luck
with your "vegable" stand.

625
00:27:46,032 --> 00:27:48,667
I'm gonna go off
and be Kenny G.

626
00:27:48,667 --> 00:27:49,702
So I'll see you later.

627
00:27:49,702 --> 00:27:52,471
Mighty neighborly
of you, Mike.

628
00:27:52,471 --> 00:27:56,042
Take her easy.

629
00:27:56,042 --> 00:27:59,578
Not much traffic
here today, huh?

630
00:27:59,578 --> 00:28:04,416
I wonder if this
was the best location
for my produce stand.

631
00:28:04,416 --> 00:28:07,987
I suppose not a lot
of people have cars here
on the Satellite of Love.

632
00:28:07,987 --> 00:28:09,722
( horn honks )

633
00:28:09,722 --> 00:28:13,325
Whoa. Hey, sounds like
I got me a potential customer

634
00:28:13,325 --> 00:28:17,029
coming down the road.

635
00:28:17,029 --> 00:28:18,831
Yeah, of course
the only guy I know

636
00:28:18,831 --> 00:28:20,232
who has a car
around these parts...

637
00:28:20,232 --> 00:28:21,801
( engine accelerating )

638
00:28:21,801 --> 00:28:23,302
Oh, no!

639
00:28:26,138 --> 00:28:29,608
Jeez, next time buy something.
( groaning )

640
00:28:30,943 --> 00:28:33,045
**

641
00:28:36,816 --> 00:28:39,218
I came here shortly
after the war.

642
00:28:39,218 --> 00:28:40,352
- What did you do?
- You okay, honey?

643
00:28:40,352 --> 00:28:42,188
Guys.

644
00:28:42,188 --> 00:28:44,090
A world
completely free

645
00:28:44,090 --> 00:28:45,524
from any kind
of outside pressure.

646
00:28:45,524 --> 00:28:47,293
Yeah, but what
do you do here?

647
00:28:47,293 --> 00:28:49,461
How do you get any kicks
in a place like this?

648
00:28:49,461 --> 00:28:51,063
Kicks?

649
00:28:51,063 --> 00:28:52,865
Kicks just keep getting
harder to find.

650
00:28:52,865 --> 00:28:54,600
I've found diversions.

651
00:28:54,600 --> 00:28:55,768
Or perversions.

652
00:28:55,768 --> 00:28:57,203
I developed a passion
for hunting.

653
00:28:57,203 --> 00:28:58,570
And Fudge Stripes.

654
00:28:58,570 --> 00:29:00,272
So that's it.
That's why the trap--

655
00:29:00,272 --> 00:29:02,074
No, no, no,
you're quite wrong.

656
00:29:02,074 --> 00:29:03,475
This island
was once the stronghold

657
00:29:03,475 --> 00:29:05,812
of some renegades
from the mainland.

658
00:29:05,812 --> 00:29:07,079
There may be one
or two of them

659
00:29:07,079 --> 00:29:08,180
still lurking
in the jungle.

660
00:29:08,180 --> 00:29:11,083
So that must have been
their handiwork.

661
00:29:11,083 --> 00:29:13,853
All these different animals,
they can't be native
to this island.

662
00:29:13,853 --> 00:29:14,854
They weren't.

663
00:29:14,854 --> 00:29:16,055
You idiot.

664
00:29:16,055 --> 00:29:17,990
But you must know
what money can accomplish.

665
00:29:17,990 --> 00:29:19,758
It's quite simple
to import

666
00:29:19,758 --> 00:29:22,228
any kind of wild beast
from anywhere in the world.

667
00:29:22,228 --> 00:29:23,796
It's a production
of Blithe Spirit.

668
00:29:23,796 --> 00:29:27,399
Other men collect
fame and riches. I collect...

669
00:29:27,399 --> 00:29:28,700
Beer cans.

670
00:29:28,700 --> 00:29:29,735
Trophies.

671
00:29:29,735 --> 00:29:30,736
Thank you.

672
00:29:30,736 --> 00:29:32,805
Man:
 Get out of my way!

673
00:29:32,805 --> 00:29:36,642
Oh, Oscar, Oscar, Oscar.

674
00:29:36,642 --> 00:29:41,413
I thought you had visitors.
I heard these strange voices.

675
00:29:41,413 --> 00:29:44,683
There's a two drunk
minimum in this film.

676
00:29:44,683 --> 00:29:46,886
These young people
stumbled onto my retreat.

677
00:29:46,886 --> 00:29:48,420
All eight of them?

678
00:29:48,420 --> 00:29:52,524
I see that you're
not feeling well again.

679
00:29:52,524 --> 00:29:55,261
That's right. Maybe...

680
00:29:55,261 --> 00:29:56,695
Paws off, pal.

681
00:29:56,695 --> 00:29:59,531
...we shouldn't bother
with introductions now, hmm?

682
00:29:59,531 --> 00:30:03,369
I'll--
I'll go back to my room.

683
00:30:03,369 --> 00:30:07,339
I think that would be best.
You can meet our young
friends later.

684
00:30:07,339 --> 00:30:09,508
I threw up on
your water buffalo.

685
00:30:09,508 --> 00:30:13,012
I hope that's okay.
( hiccups )

686
00:30:13,012 --> 00:30:15,915
'Cause you never get
a second chance to make
a first impression.

687
00:30:15,915 --> 00:30:18,650
...to meet our
young friends later.

688
00:30:18,650 --> 00:30:19,986
Yes.

689
00:30:19,986 --> 00:30:21,120
Thank you.

690
00:30:21,120 --> 00:30:24,490
I'm retir-- retir--
I'm going to sleep.

691
00:30:24,490 --> 00:30:27,326
He's an odd duck.

692
00:30:27,326 --> 00:30:29,628
Dean was once
a very brilliant man,

693
00:30:29,628 --> 00:30:32,398
but I keep him
here with me

694
00:30:32,398 --> 00:30:33,665
because I feel
sorry for him.

695
00:30:33,665 --> 00:30:35,334
And he's an excellent
chess player.

696
00:30:35,334 --> 00:30:37,836
And he looks terrific
in a gingham skirt.

697
00:30:37,836 --> 00:30:40,772
You'll all enjoy his company
when he's sober and you get
a chance to know him.

698
00:30:40,772 --> 00:30:43,742
I'm sure that's right,
but I don't think we'll
get the chance.

699
00:30:43,742 --> 00:30:46,545
We have to leave now.
Our boat's anchored
off the island.

700
00:30:46,545 --> 00:30:48,514
I'm afraid that's quite out
of the question.

701
00:30:48,514 --> 00:30:49,715
You'd never
make it through

702
00:30:49,715 --> 00:30:51,417
this animal-infested
jungle tonight.

703
00:30:51,417 --> 00:30:54,086
But our boat,
the man who owns it

704
00:30:54,086 --> 00:30:55,621
doesn't know
we went ashore.

705
00:30:55,621 --> 00:30:58,124
- He's sure to wake up.
- You can put your minds
at rest.

706
00:30:58,124 --> 00:30:59,425
I have a very
competent staff.

707
00:30:59,425 --> 00:31:01,093
They'll take care
of everything.

708
00:31:01,093 --> 00:31:02,661
We'll leave a light on.

709
00:31:02,661 --> 00:31:04,596
Will you excuse me a moment?
I'll be right back.

710
00:31:04,596 --> 00:31:06,765
I need to check my script.

711
00:31:09,735 --> 00:31:14,106
Tsk-tsk-tsk,
oh, God, look...

712
00:31:14,106 --> 00:31:16,608
Hey, everybody, guess who's
in my living room.
Robert Reed.

713
00:31:16,608 --> 00:31:20,412
Johnny, Johnny,
what are we gonna do?

714
00:31:20,412 --> 00:31:21,647
This place
gives me the creeps.

715
00:31:21,647 --> 00:31:23,082
( squeals )

716
00:31:23,082 --> 00:31:25,751
I don't know.
I kind of like
to see what gives.

717
00:31:25,751 --> 00:31:27,186
Sure, what do you think's
gonna happen

718
00:31:27,186 --> 00:31:29,488
when Tony wakes up
and finds us gone?

719
00:31:29,488 --> 00:31:31,190
He'll head straight
back to port.

720
00:31:31,190 --> 00:31:33,659
Maybe he'll remember
to report us missing.

721
00:31:33,659 --> 00:31:35,527
We're liable to be
stuck here for days.

722
00:31:35,527 --> 00:31:38,864
Don't worry about that.
Tony won't be going
anywhere without us.

723
00:31:38,864 --> 00:31:39,966
What do you mean?

724
00:31:39,966 --> 00:31:41,633
I don't know what I mean.

725
00:31:41,633 --> 00:31:44,103
I made sure of that
before we left the boat
by taking the rotor.

726
00:31:44,103 --> 00:31:46,138
He won't be able to start
the engine without us.

727
00:31:46,138 --> 00:31:49,308
Oh, Pete,
you're wonderful.

728
00:31:49,308 --> 00:31:50,943
That takes care
of the boat.

729
00:31:50,943 --> 00:31:52,878
Hold it.
He's coming back.

730
00:31:52,878 --> 00:31:56,582
I just changed out
of my smoking jacket.

731
00:31:56,582 --> 00:32:00,286
Don't even think it.

732
00:32:00,286 --> 00:32:04,790
She's got a whole lot
of face going on.

733
00:32:04,790 --> 00:32:05,958
May I present my wife?

734
00:32:05,958 --> 00:32:07,259
- Sure!
- Sure!

735
00:32:07,259 --> 00:32:09,195
Sandra, Betty,

736
00:32:09,195 --> 00:32:10,196
Bear.

737
00:32:10,196 --> 00:32:12,031
Jeanne, Johnny and Pete.

738
00:32:12,031 --> 00:32:14,633
I was sure that you would
want to make them welcome
to our island.

739
00:32:14,633 --> 00:32:16,268
Welcome to Death Island.

740
00:32:16,268 --> 00:32:19,071
Naturally, I am glad to see you.

741
00:32:19,071 --> 00:32:20,306
Did you bring Paxil?

742
00:32:20,306 --> 00:32:21,773
Will you be staying
for awhile?

743
00:32:21,773 --> 00:32:23,575
Yeah!

744
00:32:23,575 --> 00:32:25,811
- You see--
- Yes, they will, my dear.

745
00:32:25,811 --> 00:32:29,248
I think I've convinced them
of the foolishness of trying
to leave tonight.

746
00:32:29,248 --> 00:32:31,917
But we don't wanna impose.

747
00:32:31,917 --> 00:32:33,319
It is our pleasure.

748
00:32:33,319 --> 00:32:34,920
We'll make a meatloaf
of some sort.

749
00:32:34,920 --> 00:32:35,988
Isn't it, Sandra?

750
00:32:35,988 --> 00:32:37,156
Of course.

751
00:32:37,156 --> 00:32:38,690
He's working the job.

752
00:32:38,690 --> 00:32:40,392
Now if you'll show
the girls to the
guest room.

753
00:32:40,392 --> 00:32:41,793
And the guest
to the girls room.

754
00:32:41,793 --> 00:32:44,363
- Yes--
- Will you come
with me, please?

755
00:32:44,363 --> 00:32:47,299
And see what the boys
in the backroom will have.

756
00:32:47,299 --> 00:32:50,602
Eva Braun's Bed and Breakfast.

757
00:32:53,772 --> 00:32:55,441
Say, where'd you
get the braids?

758
00:32:55,441 --> 00:32:56,908
I've made arrangements for you
to spend a comfortable night.

759
00:32:56,908 --> 00:32:58,110
On me.

760
00:32:58,110 --> 00:32:59,945
We can discuss your leaving
in the morning.

761
00:32:59,945 --> 00:33:02,381
Mr. Carlin.

762
00:33:02,381 --> 00:33:05,917
Hey, Hartley.
Sorry I'm late. Idiots.

763
00:33:05,917 --> 00:33:08,720
He will take you to it.

764
00:33:08,720 --> 00:33:10,289
- I don't think--
- Good night.

765
00:33:10,289 --> 00:33:12,358
Don't let the bedbugs bite.

766
00:33:12,358 --> 00:33:14,993
Everything will be
taken care of.

767
00:33:14,993 --> 00:33:17,163
( evil laugh )

768
00:33:18,997 --> 00:33:22,801
Oh, and he has a tendency
to fondle so please be careful.

769
00:33:24,236 --> 00:33:26,172
Want turn down service?

770
00:33:27,939 --> 00:33:32,078
As for the rest of you-- oh.

771
00:33:32,078 --> 00:33:35,047
Now for some microwave
French fries.

772
00:33:35,047 --> 00:33:37,716
Well, how about me?
Do I care for a drink?

773
00:33:37,716 --> 00:33:42,488
Well, I oughtn't, but--
Well, I'll have what
I'm having.

774
00:33:42,488 --> 00:33:47,193
Ah, I love liquor.
I should gain weight so I
can handle more of this stuff.

775
00:33:48,560 --> 00:33:51,563
White shoulders.

776
00:33:51,563 --> 00:33:53,799
Later that same drink.

777
00:33:53,799 --> 00:33:55,967
- Stuff really works.
- ( laughing )

778
00:33:58,070 --> 00:34:02,374
There's some gin on
the corner of your mouth.
I got it.

779
00:34:02,374 --> 00:34:04,943
Your lips are like
crisp delicious bacon.

780
00:34:07,913 --> 00:34:12,351
We have to be careful.
He could walk in any minute.

781
00:34:12,351 --> 00:34:14,086
And there's spies
all over the house.

782
00:34:14,086 --> 00:34:15,087
I know.

783
00:34:15,087 --> 00:34:17,923
The lion may be watching.

784
00:34:17,923 --> 00:34:20,892
I can't take much more
of this madness, Sandra.

785
00:34:20,892 --> 00:34:24,029
I can't go on forever pretending
to be a useless drunk.

786
00:34:24,029 --> 00:34:26,332
Fun for a while but my liver
keeps shutting down.

787
00:34:26,332 --> 00:34:30,536
We've got to try and escape.
We've got to try again.

788
00:34:30,536 --> 00:34:32,804
But how, Dean, how?

789
00:34:32,804 --> 00:34:36,275
He'd never allow anyone
to leave this place alive.

790
00:34:36,275 --> 00:34:38,710
And we must make sure
no one ever will.

791
00:34:38,710 --> 00:34:40,212
I know.

792
00:34:40,212 --> 00:34:43,149
She's got more face
than she needs.

793
00:34:43,149 --> 00:34:46,885
All these kids here,
I think we got a chance.

794
00:34:46,885 --> 00:34:49,588
What do you mean?

795
00:34:49,588 --> 00:34:51,790
I would go out
of my mind.

796
00:34:51,790 --> 00:34:54,593
Has anyone seem-- whoa.

797
00:34:54,593 --> 00:34:57,196
I was just talking
to Mrs. Nipple-- I mean--

798
00:34:57,196 --> 00:35:00,232
Sandra, entertaining our guest?

799
00:35:00,232 --> 00:35:03,135
That's a trick question,
isn't it?

800
00:35:03,135 --> 00:35:06,004
He looks like an Easter
Island statue.

801
00:35:06,004 --> 00:35:10,409
You've recovered
from your indisposition,
I see. I'm glad.

802
00:35:10,409 --> 00:35:11,977
Thank you.

803
00:35:11,977 --> 00:35:14,546
I was just about to take
a turn around the grounds.

804
00:35:14,546 --> 00:35:16,415
Wouldn't you two like
to join me?

805
00:35:16,415 --> 00:35:18,250
You go out so seldom.

806
00:35:18,250 --> 00:35:21,587
We got this thing.

807
00:35:21,587 --> 00:35:23,922
I was about to go to my room.

808
00:35:23,922 --> 00:35:25,191
My face needs a rest.

809
00:35:25,191 --> 00:35:26,458
Dean?

810
00:35:26,458 --> 00:35:28,727
No. No, thank you.

811
00:35:28,727 --> 00:35:30,996
I was about to go
to her room, too.

812
00:35:30,996 --> 00:35:32,931
I think I'll turn in, too.

813
00:35:32,931 --> 00:35:37,102
Then you won't mind escorting
Sandra to her room, will you?

814
00:35:37,102 --> 00:35:40,672
Oh, ick. Well, all right.

815
00:35:40,672 --> 00:35:44,743
Allow me to accompany you
to my pants. Dean.

816
00:35:44,743 --> 00:35:46,178
Good night.

817
00:35:46,178 --> 00:35:47,913
How banal.

818
00:35:47,913 --> 00:35:48,914
Dean.

819
00:35:48,914 --> 00:35:50,816
I wasn't looking at her butt.

820
00:35:50,816 --> 00:35:54,019
I worked out a very satisfactory
design for living here.

821
00:35:54,019 --> 00:35:56,822
I think you realize that.

822
00:35:56,822 --> 00:36:00,726
I hope you also realize
that the presence of those
four young people here.

823
00:36:00,726 --> 00:36:03,529
Changes nothing as far
as we're concerned.

824
00:36:03,529 --> 00:36:05,697
And I'm still Victor Buono.

825
00:36:05,697 --> 00:36:09,468
I've taken care
of everything.

826
00:36:09,468 --> 00:36:12,271
I don't think he
suspects a thing.

827
00:36:21,713 --> 00:36:24,516
You don't think we're
dreaming, do you, Betty?

828
00:36:24,516 --> 00:36:25,684
It's Rob and Laura's bedroom.

829
00:36:25,684 --> 00:36:27,152
No, this is no dream.

830
00:36:27,152 --> 00:36:29,788
I can't think of a better
explanation, either.

831
00:36:29,788 --> 00:36:30,956
( knock on door )

832
00:36:30,956 --> 00:36:32,958
Hmm? Oh.

833
00:36:32,958 --> 00:36:36,528
It's the boys from the camp
across the lake.

834
00:36:36,528 --> 00:36:39,097
This knob, I love
to touch this knob.

835
00:36:39,097 --> 00:36:40,131
Come in.

836
00:36:43,235 --> 00:36:44,870
Are you all right?

837
00:36:44,870 --> 00:36:48,139
So far. I just can't understand
what's happening to us.

838
00:36:48,139 --> 00:36:49,741
Neither can I.

839
00:36:49,741 --> 00:36:52,378
That big guy that took us
to our room wasn't
very friendly.

840
00:36:52,378 --> 00:36:54,446
I've got a feeling this isn't
as friendly a place

841
00:36:54,446 --> 00:36:56,147
as that Mr. Balleau
tries to make out.

842
00:36:56,147 --> 00:36:58,284
- Shut up.
- I still can't figure out

843
00:36:58,284 --> 00:37:00,552
how it got here on the island
in the first place.

844
00:37:00,552 --> 00:37:03,755
I figure this house was first
built by one of those crackpot
millionaires

845
00:37:03,755 --> 00:37:05,190
about a hundred years ago.

846
00:37:05,190 --> 00:37:08,093
When Balleau found out
about it, he sort of moved in.

847
00:37:08,093 --> 00:37:10,696
That could be it.
He said he had a lot of money.

848
00:37:10,696 --> 00:37:14,500
But why?
I still don't know
what's going on here.

849
00:37:14,500 --> 00:37:15,634
Neither do we.

850
00:37:15,634 --> 00:37:16,968
It's a cinch nobody's
gonna tell us.

851
00:37:16,968 --> 00:37:18,937
So we'll just have to
find out for ourselves.

852
00:37:18,937 --> 00:37:21,840
- Pete: I know. But how?
- Let's split up and
have a look around.

853
00:37:21,840 --> 00:37:23,008
Yeah, but how?

854
00:37:23,008 --> 00:37:25,611
I don't know.
I'm kind of scared.

855
00:37:25,611 --> 00:37:28,680
There's nothing to be scared
about. I'll be with you.

856
00:37:28,680 --> 00:37:29,815
My point exactly.

857
00:37:29,815 --> 00:37:31,350
Betty, you'll be with me.

858
00:37:31,350 --> 00:37:35,220
Now listen, don't get into
anything you can't handle.

859
00:37:35,220 --> 00:37:39,090
If you find out anything,
head straight back here
and we'll do the same.

860
00:37:39,090 --> 00:37:40,626
Okay.

861
00:37:40,626 --> 00:37:44,229
See you at the office
on Monday then. Oh.

862
00:37:44,229 --> 00:37:47,098
Kids, let's go sell
that band candy.

863
00:37:49,968 --> 00:37:53,839
Is it time to report back yet?

864
00:37:53,839 --> 00:37:55,273
Okay, this is where we split.

865
00:37:55,273 --> 00:37:59,110
- Pete, you and Jeanne
go that way.
- Okay.

866
00:37:59,110 --> 00:38:02,080
Robert Reed's part makes it
look like he has a lid
on his head.

867
00:38:02,080 --> 00:38:05,951
I think she's shorter
than she was a minute ago.

868
00:38:09,254 --> 00:38:11,690
You have a few minutes
to take a survey.

869
00:38:11,690 --> 00:38:14,526
I promise to release you
if you swear not to cry out.

870
00:38:14,526 --> 00:38:15,994
And quit licking my hand.

871
00:38:15,994 --> 00:38:18,997
All right, go back to your room.

872
00:38:18,997 --> 00:38:20,499
We'll go with you.

873
00:38:20,499 --> 00:38:22,568
We've got to talk to you.

874
00:38:22,568 --> 00:38:23,735
What's this all about?

875
00:38:23,735 --> 00:38:26,338
Please go back to your room.
We cannot talk here.

876
00:38:26,338 --> 00:38:28,474
Please. There is death
all around us.

877
00:38:28,474 --> 00:38:30,409
You could crack a window.

878
00:38:30,409 --> 00:38:32,210
Okay, Betty, come on.

879
00:38:32,210 --> 00:38:33,845
Hurry.

880
00:38:33,845 --> 00:38:38,550
They progressed a full ten feet
before being captured.

881
00:38:38,550 --> 00:38:43,088
Be careful. There's death all
around us in here, too.

882
00:38:43,088 --> 00:38:46,725
We don't have money
for other sets.

883
00:38:46,725 --> 00:38:48,927
Well?

884
00:38:48,927 --> 00:38:49,961
Something I wanna tell you.

885
00:38:49,961 --> 00:38:51,497
Radishes make me burp.

886
00:38:51,497 --> 00:38:53,932
Unless you do exactly
as I say, you'll never
get off this island.

887
00:38:53,932 --> 00:38:55,534
There, I've said my peace.

888
00:38:55,534 --> 00:38:59,270
Looks like Liberace's in there.

889
00:39:00,506 --> 00:39:03,709
Indiana Nerd
and the Temple of Dork.

890
00:39:03,709 --> 00:39:06,445
( humming )

891
00:39:06,445 --> 00:39:09,981
Am I waiting okay?
Or should I-- wait--
How's this?

892
00:39:09,981 --> 00:39:11,282
I'm waiting.

893
00:39:16,087 --> 00:39:20,526
Both: Surprise!
* Happy Birthday to you *

894
00:39:20,526 --> 00:39:24,530
There's a whole 'nother
room in there.

895
00:39:24,530 --> 00:39:26,998
There's a stairway there
that leads down to some
kind of a tunnel.

896
00:39:26,998 --> 00:39:28,066
So?

897
00:39:28,066 --> 00:39:30,235
Come on, let's see
where it goes.

898
00:39:32,638 --> 00:39:34,305
Please listen to him.

899
00:39:34,305 --> 00:39:37,809
All wight, I'm wistening.

900
00:39:37,809 --> 00:39:39,310
I don't understand any of this.

901
00:39:39,310 --> 00:39:42,013
I'll try and explain it
as quickly as I can.

902
00:39:42,013 --> 00:39:44,182
Grab your friends from
the other room in here.

903
00:39:44,182 --> 00:39:45,517
- They're not there.
- What?

904
00:39:45,517 --> 00:39:47,352
Well, they're out
in the house somewhere.

905
00:39:47,352 --> 00:39:48,454
They're trying to find out
what's going on.

906
00:39:48,454 --> 00:39:50,489
That's where we were
when you stopped us.

907
00:39:50,489 --> 00:39:52,323
That's bad.

908
00:39:52,323 --> 00:39:54,059
- I'll go look for them.
- No, stay here.

909
00:39:54,059 --> 00:39:57,796
One of the stupidest hostages
we've ever had here.

910
00:39:57,796 --> 00:40:00,632
Let's just pray
they'll be all right.

911
00:40:00,632 --> 00:40:03,168
This is where they kneel down
with Kissinger.

912
00:40:06,472 --> 00:40:10,041
What do you know?
A microbrewery. Of
course I can't drink.

913
00:40:10,041 --> 00:40:12,778
An entire cave made out
of garbage bags.

914
00:40:15,113 --> 00:40:16,848
Are we over-investigating?

915
00:40:16,848 --> 00:40:20,251
We need to ask ourselves that.
We should probably hide
under the bed.

916
00:40:20,251 --> 00:40:22,420
You're constantly
clammy, Steve.

917
00:40:26,224 --> 00:40:27,626
( screams )

918
00:40:27,626 --> 00:40:30,361
Cadavers for Algernon.

919
00:40:30,361 --> 00:40:34,432
She's finally the size five
she always wanted to be.

920
00:40:34,432 --> 00:40:37,135
Ow, Susan, you're
squeezing my booster shot.

921
00:40:40,539 --> 00:40:43,875
Come on, it's just a
Jaycees haunted house.

922
00:40:45,210 --> 00:40:49,280
The wall is covered
with Issey Miyake clothing.

923
00:40:49,280 --> 00:40:52,651
- Steve Allen, PI.
- Hmm?

924
00:40:55,120 --> 00:40:57,823
I know what. We'll throw
Gatorade on 'em.

925
00:41:01,693 --> 00:41:05,330
Okay, Mr. Villechaize.
We'll get you back
to your room here.

926
00:41:07,633 --> 00:41:11,069
I wonder what he scores
on the Myers-Briggs Inventory.

927
00:41:13,672 --> 00:41:18,309
Okay, I've decided that phase
"B" of our plan involves
a lot of hiding.

928
00:41:22,380 --> 00:41:25,250
I love this job.

929
00:41:25,250 --> 00:41:26,785
I can dress how I want.

930
00:41:27,953 --> 00:41:30,188
Gulp.

931
00:41:30,188 --> 00:41:34,059
Huh? When did I have
teenagers installed?

932
00:41:36,728 --> 00:41:40,165
Oh, man, getting caught
would have been really awkward

933
00:41:40,165 --> 00:41:42,701
'cause they haven't
been introduced.

934
00:41:42,701 --> 00:41:44,703
This would be
really suspenseful,

935
00:41:44,703 --> 00:41:47,438
but for the fact
that it isn't.

936
00:41:47,438 --> 00:41:51,276
Oh, no, they've stumbled
onto another room.

937
00:41:52,878 --> 00:41:55,446
There's that air hockey game
we gave him that he never uses

938
00:41:55,446 --> 00:41:57,282
and a rowing machine.

939
00:41:59,517 --> 00:42:01,519
Oh, it's their
cotton candy lab.

940
00:42:04,522 --> 00:42:07,959
Are we having
the clambake or not?

941
00:42:07,959 --> 00:42:09,861
Quick, into the vat.

942
00:42:13,932 --> 00:42:15,667
He's changing
the kitty litter.

943
00:42:17,335 --> 00:42:18,837
Nice back porch.

944
00:42:18,837 --> 00:42:21,406
Nipple.

945
00:42:23,041 --> 00:42:25,611
Hi, again.

946
00:42:25,611 --> 00:42:28,346
Employees must wash hands
before leaving dungeon.

947
00:42:31,216 --> 00:42:34,119
Claussen Kosher Ladies.
Never cooked.

948
00:42:34,119 --> 00:42:36,321
Always crisp.

949
00:42:36,321 --> 00:42:39,290
I gotta limit myself
to about three seconds
of looking at that.

950
00:42:41,559 --> 00:42:46,364
Time to rinse out
my silk things.

951
00:42:46,364 --> 00:42:50,135
Man, if it weren't for
my hobbies, I'd go nuts.

952
00:42:50,135 --> 00:42:52,537
Gonna make some of his
famous toe jam.

953
00:42:52,537 --> 00:42:54,973
- Oh, that's just so wrong.
- ( spits )

954
00:42:54,973 --> 00:42:56,107
Sorry.

955
00:42:59,144 --> 00:43:01,079
Oh, it shrunk.

956
00:43:03,782 --> 00:43:07,118
Went a little too far
with the dermabrasion.

957
00:43:07,118 --> 00:43:11,690
I usually don't look good
in flesh tones, but...

958
00:43:11,690 --> 00:43:14,559
That's funny.

959
00:43:14,559 --> 00:43:16,828
Jackie Mason, what are
you doing with my head?

960
00:43:16,828 --> 00:43:19,230
This head should not be here.
It should be on Broadway.

961
00:43:19,230 --> 00:43:22,167
This is one of Jeff Smith's
weirder shows.

962
00:43:22,167 --> 00:43:24,102
It's fun to watch
a craft goon at work.

963
00:43:24,102 --> 00:43:26,204
Mm-hmm.

964
00:43:26,204 --> 00:43:28,940
Here we go, just a
little more, there.

965
00:43:28,940 --> 00:43:31,576
One of my best ones
if I do say so myself.

966
00:43:33,111 --> 00:43:35,981
Now be sure to save
the giblets for the calf.

967
00:43:35,981 --> 00:43:37,816
The Basques are one
of the finest people.

968
00:43:37,816 --> 00:43:40,819
They do wonderful things
with organ meat.

969
00:43:40,819 --> 00:43:42,453
This is too fattening.

970
00:43:42,453 --> 00:43:45,490
I'll get rid of it
so I won't be tempted.

971
00:43:45,490 --> 00:43:48,827
Now to go crunch
some numbers.

972
00:43:48,827 --> 00:43:52,530
Long before Peter Billingsley,
there was this guy.

973
00:43:54,132 --> 00:43:58,003
We are not staying for dinner.

974
00:43:58,003 --> 00:44:00,939
- Would you--
- I can't go!

975
00:44:00,939 --> 00:44:04,609
I can't believe it.
It's just too awful.

976
00:44:04,609 --> 00:44:05,777
Poor Lani.

977
00:44:05,777 --> 00:44:08,113
Every word is true.
I swear it.

978
00:44:08,113 --> 00:44:11,349
-You have to believe us.
- ( knocking )

979
00:44:11,349 --> 00:44:13,885
Not a word about this.

980
00:44:13,885 --> 00:44:15,086
Somebody lend me a hand.

981
00:44:15,086 --> 00:44:17,622
Bad news. The kitchen's
not up to code.

982
